\begin{abstract}
The fundamental importance of water for many chemical processes has motivated the development of countless efficient but approximate water potentials for largescale molecular dynamics simulations, from simple empirical force fields to very sophisticated flexible water models. Accurate and generally applicable water potentials should fulfill a number of requirements. They should have a quality close to quantum chemical methods, they should explicitly depend on all degrees of freedom including all relevant manybody interactions, and they should be able to describe molecular dissociation and recombination. In this work, we present a high-dimensional neural network (NN) potential for
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/8af6c61f-a5d3-40d8-956b-15649ba06048-01.jpg?height=449&width=842&top_left_y=737&top_left_x=1092)
water clusters based on density-functional theory (DFT) calculations, which is constructed using clusters containing up to 10 monomers and is in principle able to meet all these requirements. We investigate the reliability of specific parametrizations employing two frequently used generalized gradient approximation (GGA) exchange-correlation functionals, PBE and RPBE, as reference methods. We find that the binding energy errors of the NN potentials with respect to DFT are significantly lower than the typical uncertainties of DFT calculations arising from the choice of the exchange-correlation functional. Further, we examine the role of van der Waals interactions, which are not properly described by GGA functionals. Specifically, we incorporate the D3 scheme suggested by Grimme (J. Chem. Phys. 2010, 132, 154104) in our potentials and demonstrate that it can be applied to GGA-based NN potentials in the same way as to DFT calculations without modification. Our results show that the description of small water clusters provided by the RPBE functional is significantly improved if van der Waals interactions are included, while in case of the PBE functional, which is well-known to yield stronger binding than RPBE, van der Waals corrections lead to overestimated binding energies.
\end{abstract}

\section*{- INTRODUCTION}

Understanding the properties of water is of crucial importance in many fields of science, from biological systems to electrochemisty and corrosion. \({ }^{1,2}\) Computer simulations can provide valuable insights provided that a reliable description of the atomic interactions is available. While high-quality results can be obtained by first-principles methods, e.g., in the form of ab initio molecular dynamics (MD) simulations based on density-functional theory (DFT), \({ }^{3,4}\) a direct application of electronic structure calculations on-the-fly is computationally very demanding for extended simulations of large systems. Consequently, the development of more efficient interatomic potentials is a very active field of research.

The starting point for the construction of any interatomic potential is the dependence of the Hamiltonian on the positions and charges of the nuclei as well as on the total number of electrons in the system. Since, in turn, the Hamiltonian completely defines the energy, a very complicated and generally unknown high-dimensional function, the potential-energy surface (PES), exists, which yields the total energy of a system if the atomic structure is provided. If an efficient way could be found to construct this function analytically without solving the Schrödinger equation again and again for any new atomic configuration, highly efficient MD
simulations could be carried out. There are two fundamentally different approaches to obtain such analytic PESs. One possibility is to derive a simplified PES by introducing a series of more or less drastic physical approximations leading in the extreme case to simple classical force fields. Alternatively, a set of electronic structure calculations can be carried out at the highest affordable quality level for a number of representative atomic configurations, which is then fitted by very flexible mathematical functions to approximate the unknown PES as accurately as possible.

In the case of water, many types of interactions contribute to the total shape of the PES: polar covalent bonds within the water monomers, hydrogen-bonds between nearest neighbor molecules, long-range electrostatic forces, and van der Waals interactions. In the most basic models for water, these contributions are completely separated and treated by simple analytic terms. In the most efficient water force fields, the internal degrees of freedom of the individual water molecules

