are even completely eliminated by freezing the monomer geometries, e.g., in the popular TIP4P \({ }^{5}\) or SPC/E \({ }^{6}\) water potentials. More sophisticated water force fields include flexible monomers and/or polarization, \({ }^{7-9}\) and in a few force fields it is even possible to make and break chemical bonds, \({ }^{10-12}\) i.e., to dissociate and recombine water.

Most of the commonly used water force fields have been parametrized to reproduce important experimentally determined physical properties of liquid water. Therefore, these potentials often have a limited transferability, i.e., they are not equally well applicable to different phases of water like the bulk liquid, ice or isolated clusters. An unbiased approach to construct water potentials can be based on high-level ab initio reference data yielding so-called "ab initio potentials". Many accurate ab initio water potentials have been reported in the literature. \({ }^{13-21}\) A comprehensive summary of even the most important water potentials is beyond the scope of this paper, and the interested reader is referred to a number of excellent reviews. \({ }^{5,22-24}\)

In the present work, we investigate the applicability of artificial neural networks (NNs), \({ }^{25}\) a very flexible mathematical class of functions, to represent the PES of small water clusters using DFT as reference method. NNs are ideal candidates for constructing PESs as it has been proven that they are in principle able to approximate any unknown function with arbitrary accuracy. \({ }^{26}\) Although DFT is not the optimum choice for the description of the properties of water clusters, the topology of the DFT PES is very similar to the PES of higherlevel wave function methods. Further, DFT offers the best compromise between accuracy and efficiency. Therefore, DFT is a suitable benchmark method for the primary aim of the present work, which is to show that complex first-principles PESs of water clusters can be described by NN potentials without a significant reduction of accuracy. This work represents an intermediate step toward the construction of an NN PES that is equally applicable to clusters, liquid water, and ice.

In recent years, NN potentials have been constructed for a number of systems \({ }^{27,28}\) including water. Surprisingly, only two NN potentials for single fully flexible water molecules have been reported to date. \({ }^{29,30}\) For all NN potentials applicable to more than one water molecule, the internal structure of the monomers has been frozen. In 1997, No et al. presented a NN potential for the water dimer, \({ }^{31}\) and this work has later been extended to include polarization energies in the TIP4P water model. \({ }^{32}\) While this potential is applicable to bulk water, still many of the limitations of the TIP4P model are present. In 1998, Gassner et al. reported a similar potential, \({ }^{33}\) which was further extended to describe three-body interactions in the solvation of \(\mathrm{Al}^{3+}\) ions. While also here the internal monomer degrees of freedom have not been included, this work still represents a major conceptual improvement since the permutation symmetry of chemically equivalent atoms has been considered explicitly. Another possibility to include the polarization of water has been suggested by Handley and Popelier. \({ }^{34,35}\) In their work, atom-centered multipoles are fitted to the electron density using NNs, which allows one to calculate the electrostatic contribution to the total energy. In spite of all this work, no full-dimensional and reactive NN PES for systems containing several water molecules has been reported to date.

Building on our previous work, \({ }^{36,37}\) here we present a NN potential for water clusters covering the range from the
monomer to the decamer. Two different functionals, \(\mathrm{PBE}^{38}\) and RPBE, \({ }^{39}\) have been used to describe electronic exchange and correlation in the DFT reference calculations. This enables one to compare the uncertainty of the PES resulting from the choice of the exchange-correlation functional and the errors arising from fitting the NNs.

A critical aspect of any PES for water is the description of nonbonded interactions. It is well-known that dispersion (van der Waals) interactions are not correctly included in DFT calculations employing generalized gradient approximation (GGA) functionals, and a number of methods have been proposed to overcome this limitation, \({ }^{40}\) from van der Waals density functionals \({ }^{41}\) via atom-density based corrections \({ }^{42}\) to dispersion corrected pseudo potentials. \({ }^{43}\) However, instead of applying a correction to the DFT data before constructing the NN PESs, we follow a different approach using a scheme suggested by Grimme, \({ }^{44}\) which introduces a simple additive correction to the energy and forces. Since NN potentials can reproduce DFT PESs numerically very accurately, the Grimme scheme can also be applied directly to the NN energies and forces without modification. We find that the van der Waalscorrected NN PES based on the RPBE functional is of excellent quality and very close to coupled cluster calculations, while the NN PES constructed using the PBE functional shows strong overbinding. This different behavior, which is in good agreement with previous DFT work of Tonigold and Groß, \({ }^{45}\) is a consequence of well-known strong binding present in the case of the PBE functional, which is further increased by adding attractive van der Waals corrections. On the other hand, in the case of the RPBE functional, which generally yields weaker binding energies for water, adding attractive interactions yields an improved description of the PES.

\section*{- HIGH-DIMENSIONAL NN POTENTIALS}

In the past two decades, artificial NNs have been used by a number of groups to construct very accurate PESs for molecules in the gas phase \({ }^{30,46-51}\) and molecules interacting with metal surfaces. \({ }^{52-57}\) Different NN approaches have been developed for this purpose, and several types of electronic structure calculations from DFT to high-level wave function methods have been used as a reference to determine the fitting parameters of the NNs.

In the present work we employ the high-dimensional NN method suggested by Behler and Parrinello, \({ }^{58,59}\) which has recently been extended by explicit long-range electrostatic interactions, \({ }^{36,60}\) an important prerequisite for applications to polar solvents like water. The method has been described in detail elsewhere, and here we just provide a concise summary of its essential features. The total energy of a system containing \(N\) atoms is given by
\[
E_{\text {tot }}=E_{\text {short }}+E_{\text {elec }}
\]
as a sum of a "short range" energy \(E_{\text {short }}\) and the electrostatic energy \(E_{\text {elec }} \cdot E_{\text {short }}\) is a sum of environment-dependent atomic energy contributions \(E_{i}\)
\[
E_{\text {short }}=\sum_{i=1}^{N} E_{i}
\]

The local atomic environments are defined by a cutoff radius, which is typically between 6 and \(10 \AA\), and the positions of all neighboring atoms inside this cutoff sphere enter the atomic energies. The coordinates of the neighbors are described by a
set of atom-centered many-body symmetry functions, \({ }^{59}\) which serves as input vector for an atomic feed-forward NN yielding the respective \(E_{i}\) as output. For training the weight parameters of the NNs, first the short-range energies and forces need to be extracted from the DFT data. This is done by removing the electrostatic energies and forces, which are calculated using environment-dependent charges \(q_{i}\) employing a second set of atomic NNs. For this purpose, DFT reference charges are used. There are many partitioning methods available in the literature to estimate atomic charges, and in the present work we use the Hirshfeld method. \({ }^{61}\)

Formally, the electrostatic energies can become very large in value for short interatomic distances. Consequently, to avoid numerically unfavorable large variations in the resulting shortrange energies, the electrostatic energies and forces are screened for short interatomic distances \(r_{i j}\) by the screening function
\[
f_{\text {screen }}\left(r_{i j}\right)=\left\{\begin{array}{cl}
\frac{1}{2}\left[1-\cos \left(\frac{\pi \cdot r_{i j}}{r_{\mathrm{s}}}\right)\right] & \text { for } r_{i j} \leq r_{\mathrm{s}} \\
1 & \text { for } r_{i j}>r_{\mathrm{s}}
\end{array}\right.
\]

The parameter \(r_{\mathrm{s}}\) determines the range of the screening function, and the electrostatic energy of a nonperiodic system like a water cluster is then given by a screened Coulomb's law,
\[
E_{\mathrm{elec}}=\sum_{i=1}^{N-1} \sum_{j>i}^{N} \frac{q_{i} q_{j}}{r_{i j}} \cdot f_{\text {screen }}\left(r_{i j}\right)
\]

Screening the electrostatic interactions at short interatomic distances does not introduce errors in the NN PES, since the missing parts of the electrostatic energies and forces are automatically compensated by the remaining short-range components ensuring that the total energy is conserved. For the same reason, the rather short electrostatic screening radii do not result in artifacts in the forces, which otherwise could give rise to problems related to energy conservation in MD simulations. The introduction of explicit electrostatics in the NN potential is mainly relevant for including long-range interactions, which cannot be represented by the short-range atomic energies due to the employed symmetry function cutoff. Even in the case of periodic systems, in our approach electrostatic interactions are not truncated, but can be included via standard Ewald summation techniques. \({ }^{62}\) Further details on the high-dimensional NN method, which has been applied to a number of systems, \({ }^{63-70}\) can be found elsewhere. \({ }^{36,58-60}\)

The accuracy of NN potentials is limited by the reliability of the underlying reference electronic structure calculations. One of the most critical limitations of GGA exchange-correlation functionals is the inability to take van der Waals interactions into account correctly. A family of methods, which is particularly easy to use, has been suggested by Grimme. \(44,71,72\)
For the present work, this approach is very appealing since it can be applied to the final GGA-based NN PES in the same way as to the original underlying DFT reference data. Therefore, it is not necessary to include van der Waals interactions already in the training data of the NN, although this would also be possible. Instead, once constructed, a NN potential based on plain GGA data can be used in applications with and without van der Waals corrections at basically no additional computational costs, as the pair potentials of the Grimme scheme are computationally less demanding than the
evaluation of the symmetry functions and NN atomic energies, and as only a single NN PES needs to be fitted to enable simulations with and without dispersion corrections.

Specifically, in the present work we extend the NN potential for water using Grimme's D3 method. \({ }^{44}\) The van der Waals corrected NN energy is
\[
E_{\mathrm{tot}}=E_{\mathrm{short}}+E_{\mathrm{elec}}+E_{\mathrm{disp}}
\]

The expression for the dispersion correction energy \(E_{\text {disp }}\) is given in Grimme's original work \({ }^{44}\) including the parameters for various exchange-correlation functionals. Finally, we remark that the inclusion of dispersion interactions is not required due to limitations of the NN approach, which would be well able to describe these interactions via the short-range energies still having a notable range. Instead, the inclusion of \(E_{\text {disp }}\) is merely a consequence of the limitations of the specific reference method chosen in the present work.

\section*{- COMPUTATIONAL DETAILS}

The reference DFT calculations to construct the NN potentials have been carried out using the FHI-aims program, \({ }^{73}\) an allatom electron code employing numeric atom-centered basis functions. For the chosen "tier 2" basis set, the basis incompleteness and the basis set superposition errors have been carefully checked. They are in the order of \(3 \mathrm{meV} / \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}\) for binding energies and thus are much smaller than typical hydrogen bonds determining the structure of the water clusters. All other parameters of the calculation, such as integration grids and radial basis function cutoffs, have been converged to ensure energy errors of less than \(1 \mathrm{meV} / \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}\).
The reference structures to train the NNs were generated systematically in an iterative process by alternating improvements of the NN potentials and replica-exchange MD (REMD) simulations \({ }^{74}\) based on preliminary NN potentials to suggest new structures. For this purpose TiReX, \({ }^{75}\) a parallel driver based on the TINKER molecular modeling package, \({ }^{76}\) has been used. The integration time step was 0.5 fs , and swap attempts between replicas were made every 100 fs . We used eight replicas at eight different temperatures, linearly distributed between 50 and 250 K , and the Bussi-Donadio-Parrinello thermostat \({ }^{77}\) to control the temperature. Further details on the systematic construction of the training set can be found elsewhere. \({ }^{36,37}\) The finally obtained reference structures were split randomly into a training set ( \(90 \%\) ) and an independent test set (10\%), which allows estimating the accuracy of the NN PESs for structures not included in the training set.

The NN potentials were constructed using our NN program RuNNer. \({ }^{78}\) The atomic NNs contain two hidden layers with 70 nodes each for the short-range and for the electrostatic part. Nineteen and 21 symmetry functions were used to describe the atomic environment of the hydrogen and the oxygen atoms, respectively. The specific parameters defining the symmetry functions are the same for the short-range and the long-range part and can be found in ref 37. The cutoff radii for the symmetry functions and for the screening function have been tested systematically, and we found a value of \(10 \AA\) to provide very accurate potentials. The weight parameters were optimized iteratively using the global extended Kalman filter algorithm \({ }^{79}\) after applying the normalization scheme of Nguyen and Widrow. \({ }^{80}\)

