\section*{- RESULTS}

Reference Configurations. The composition of the reference data sets used to train the NNs is of crucial importance for the accuracy and reliability of the resulting potentials. It has been suggested to construct training sets in a systematic way employing MD simulations to sample relevant configurations. \({ }^{47,65,81}\) This method is not self-starting and requires a preliminary potential fitted to an initial set of structures. Here, we follow a similar method described in ref 37 for smaller water clusters up to the hexamer. Specifically, the initial data sets were generated using random clusters and subsequently improved by adding structures sampled in REMD simulations. In the present work we extend this data set in the same way to larger water clusters by including additional reference structures for \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}\right)_{n=7-10}\). After having obtained a sufficiently large set of reference structures from MD trajectories using preliminary potentials, the random configurations were excluded from the data set since they typically
represent high-energy configurations that are not relevant for applications at chemically meaningful temperatures.

Table 1 shows the root mean squared error (RMSE) for energies, forces, and charges using the final NN potentials separately for each cluster size as well as the final training set composition. The energy error is in the order of \(2 \mathrm{meV} / \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}\) for all clusters, which is about a factor of 2 smaller than the RMSE obtained with the previous potential for \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}\right)_{n=3-6 \cdot}{ }^{37}\) This significant improvement of the accuracy is a consequence of the more realistic composition of the reference data set. Even though the randomly generated configurations were removed, the data set still includes many distorted structures with high binding energies, as can be seen from the regression plots in Figure 1 (PBE) and Figure 2 (RPBE). For both functionals, the NN potentials are based on data covering approximately the same binding energy range of \(800 \mathrm{meV} / \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}\). The RMSEs of the forces and charges are \(60 \mathrm{meV} / \AA\) and \(0.002 e\), respectively, and thus very small.

Structural and Energetic Properties of the Clusters. In order to examine the quality of the constructed NN potentials in more detail, we located a number of local and global minima on the DFT and NN PESs for water clusters up to the decamer. The obtained binding energies and geometries have been analyzed and compared to the results of recent high-level quantum chemical calculations. Figure 3 shows the investigated low-lying minima of \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}\right)_{n=2-10}\). Initial structures were taken from ref 82 and fully optimized with the L-BFGS algorithm. \({ }^{83}\) Binding energies and average adjacent oxygen-oxygen distances for all structures are listed in Tables 2 and 3.

First we focus on the comparison between the NN potentials and the DFT reference data without van der Waals corrections.

