Having confirmed that the NN potentials are in quantitative agreement with the underlying reference DFT calculations, we now turn to the results for the van der Waals corrected NN potentials. Standard gradient-based DFT functionals, such as PBE and RPBE, cannot correctly describe van der Waals forces, which are important for many systems such as water clusters. Santra et al. have shown that several popular functionals fail to predict the correct energetic ordering of low-lying hexamer isomers. Moreover, none of the investigated functionals was able to predict the correct global minimum. \({ }^{84}\) However, augmenting DFT calculations with van der Waals correction schemes leads to significantly improved results. \({ }^{84,85}\) The same observations have been made for the NN potentials concerning the isomers of the hexamer. In combination with the D3 method, the PBE and the RPBE-based NN potentials both predict the Prism isomer to be the global minimum configuration, which is in agreement with recent CCSD(T) calculations extrapolated to the complete basis set (CBS) limit performed by Bates and Tschumper \({ }^{86}\) and Temelso et al. \({ }^{82}\) As shown in Figure 4 also the energetic ordering Prism < Cage < Book-1 < Book-2 < Cyclic < Bag is consistent with their results. The best agreement of the relative binding energies is found for the NN(RPBE)-D3 potential, while for NN(PBE)-D3 the Prism, Cage, and Book isomers all have too close energies within

