Finally, we note that, although NNs enable a very reliable representation of first-principles PESs, the energy differences between several isomers are very small and beyond the accuracy of most electronic structure calculations. Further, in MD simulations at typical temperatures, frequent transitions will occurr between such structures leading to significant populations of several isomers.

\section*{- DISCUSSION}

The results presented above show that NN potentials are able to describe water clusters up to the decamer with excellent accuracy, which has been the main goal of this work. Another interesting question is whether the present NN parametrizations are also applicable to larger water clusters. In the case of simple water force fields, this is not a problem since their functional form usually prevents spectacular failures while it also does not allow one to achieve a very high numerical accuracy. Water potentials using truncated many-body expansions are also applicable to larger systems. \({ }^{13}\) In the case of NN potentials, this cannot be taken for granted. The functional form of NNs is extremely flexible to enable a close numerical agreement with the electronic structure reference data. The price to pay is a large number of required training structures and limited extrapolation capabilities. Still, an advantage of using NNs is that whenever situations are found in which the NN is not reliable, the potential can be improved by adding additional training data without requiring changes in the functional form.
We have tested our NN PESs for both functionals for water clusters containing up to 16 monomers. The initial configurations for \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}\right)_{n=11-16}\) were taken from the work of Su et al. \({ }^{87}\) who performed geometry optimizations with the X3LYP functional. They are shown in Figure 7. In Table 4, the

