In general, the reason for the limited transferability is the description of the cluster structures in form of many-body symmetry functions. These contain additive terms growing with the number of neighbors. \({ }^{59}\) Consequently, for large numbers of monomers, the set of symmetry function values is outside the training region. This can easily be detected in practical applications, and an extension of the range of validity of the NN PES is straightforward. Once the number of monomers in the training structures is sufficiently large to fill the cutoff spheres defining the symmetry functions, the NN PESs will become applicable to bulk water without extrapolation. Work to reach this goal is currently in progress. We note that, in general, the discussed extrapolation phenomenon strongly depends on the choice of input coordinates for the NN. The high-dimensional NN method of Behler and Parrinello has been designed for large systems, and, if trained to bulk systems, this problem is not present, since for periodic systems the cutoff spheres are automatically filled. Thus, the most efficient way to construct a NN PES for bulk water would be to directly use bulk training data. Alternatively, it would be possible to use truncated many-body expansions, as has been proposed by Carrington and co-workers \({ }^{51}\) and also by Raff \({ }^{88}\) for other systems. Further, an NN adaption of the coordinates used by Bowman and co-workers \({ }^{13}\) would be feasible.

Finally, some conclusions on the general applicability of NNs to construct PESs can be made. NNs are numerically very accurate and belong to the most precise functions for data fitting. This has been confirmed by the present work and many other potentials in the literature. Still, due to their very flexible functional form they can be unreliable in chemical situations which are very different from the structures present in the data set. This can and should be checked when NN potentials are used. The number of degrees of freedom that can be included depends on the particular choice of the NN type. The total energy expression of our high-dimensional NN scheme depends explicitly on the positions of all atoms in the system, but chemically irrelevant interactions are removed between distant atoms by employing cutoff functions. Consequently, the cutoff radius of the symmetry functions is a convergence parameter that needs to be tested and increased until the remaining error of the PES has converged to a sufficiently small value. By transforming the Cartesian coordinates of the atoms to many-body symmetry functions depending simultaneously on the positions of all neighboring atoms, \({ }^{59}\) even very high
order many-body interactions are taken into account. Further, NN potentials are reactive in the sense that in contrast to most force fields, no bonds and atom types need to be specified. Like in electronic structure calculations, the energies and forces are calculated as a function of the atomic positions and chemical elements only. In principle, this enables one to study proton transport in water using NN potentials, although also the training set then needs to include structures along the reaction pathways, which is not yet the case in our work. An additional advantage of the presented NN method is its extreme efficiency compared with electronic structure calculations. On current computers, NN potentials are able to provide the energy and forces of at least 100 atoms per second and core, and the method is well suited for parallelization. This enables MD simulations of systems containing thousands of atoms on a routine basis. Also, an extension of our NN method to include further species is possible without any changes in the functional form, e.g., to include solvated ions or metal surfaces.

\section*{- CONCLUSION}

A high-dimensional NN potential for water clusters up to the decamer has been constructed using DFT calculations and two different exchange-correlation functionals, PBE and RPBE. The resulting NN PESs are in excellent agreement with the underlying DFT data for both functionals. The RMSE values of the energies and forces are extremely small: they are approximately 2 meV per monomer for the binding energies and about \(60 \mathrm{meV} / \AA\) for the forces. Also the DFT Hirshfeld charges are reproduced with a very small error of about \(0.002 e\). To overcome present limitations of GGA functionals in the description of van der Waals interactions, the NN total energy expression can be extended by a correction term taking dispersion interactions into account. Specifically, we have shown that the D3 method suggested by Grimme can be added in a straightforward way to NN PESs derived for plain GGA data.

Of all investigated cluster geometries, only the prism structure of the hexamer has not been found in geometry optimizations employing the NN potentials. This is not unexpected, because the hexamer is notoriously difficult to describe due to many energetically close local minima. Nevertheless, all global minima are correctly predicted by DFT and the NN PESs. In accordance with previous studies carried out by other groups, we find that including van der Waals corrections leads to an overall improved quality of the DFT PES in the case of the RPBE functional, while the PBE functional provides better binding energies without dispersion corrections. The same trends have been found for the NN PESs, since they are numerically extremely close to the DFT data. A drawback of the current parametrizations based on rather small clusters is the limited extrapolation capability to larger clusters, which are not present in the training set. This deficiency can be removed by adding larger clusters to the training sets yielding converged PESs applicable to bulk water. This will be confirmed by work currently in progress.

